  simplicity; email batching has to track what was already sent per user; and the inbox query gets steadily more
  expensive.

  [1m3. No event log — write notifications directly.[0m Service functions create [38;5;153mNotification[39m rows inline. Fewest tables,
  least indirection.
  [3mBuys:[0m the least code by a clear margin, and the easiest thing to debug.
  [3mCosts:[0m no activity feed and no replay — if a notification bug ships, the information to regenerate correct
  notifications is gone. Notification text gets baked in at write time, so adding a channel or changing wording can't be
  applied retroactively.

  [1mMy recommendation is 1.[0m Fan-out on write is the boring, well-worn choice, and the write amplification it's criticized
  for is irrelevant at 5–50 users. More importantly, it's the only one where the event log stays authoritative [3mand[0m
  per-item read state is cheap — and read state is exactly where 2 and 3 quietly accumulate the complexity they claimed
  to avoid. The activity feed falling out for free is a real bonus for a shared task tool: "who changed this and when"
  is usually the next thing someone asks.

  Worth flagging: 1 and 2 are not hard to switch between later, since both keep the event log. Approach 3 is the one-way
  door — it discards the information the other two are built on.
